WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.250
A beloved sci-fi actor, a cult-favorite comedian,
and a "Talladega Nights" standout all lost

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:11.470
their lives in 2021 – and the news may have
gone under your radar.

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:15.000
These are the actors you might not know passed
away last year.

00:00:15.221 --> 00:00:20.680
Croatian-American actress Mira Furlan, best
known for her stints on Babylon 5 and Lost,

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.650
died in January 2021.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.980
Her death was announced on her official Twitter
account.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.930
The cause of death was not revealed.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.179
She was 56.

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:35.160
Born in Zagreb, Furlan and her husband emigrated
to America in the early '90s amid rising tensions

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.910
in their home country, settling in New York
City.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:42.600
Three years after the move, she landed what
would become her most iconic role: Minbari

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.100
Ambassador Delenn in Babylon 5.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.649
She briefly returned to Croatia before jetting
off to Hawaii to play the part of Danielle

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:51.000
Rousseau in Lost.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.910
She appeared as the French loner in 20 episodes
of ABC's hit show.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:01:02.019
"You offered to fix my music box after all
I've done to you, striking you, shocking you."

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.680
Actor Frank Bonner, best known for playing
the spectacularly dressed Herb Tarlek on the

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:13.350
classic sitcom "WKRP in Cincinnati," died
in June 2021, according to TMZ.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.280
He was 79.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.040
His daughter broke the news, posting on the
official "WKRP" Facebook page,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:25.477
"He loved his fans and was still signing autograph
requests up until the last few weeks of his illness.

00:01:25.477 --> 00:01:27.655
Thank you to all who followed his career.

00:01:27.655 --> 00:01:29.105
He will be forever missed."

00:01:29.105 --> 00:01:32.656
"I love you…I really do."

00:01:32.808 --> 00:01:37.990
Bonner made his first onscreen appearance
in the 1967 horror film "The Equinox ...A

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:39.479
Journey Into the Supernatural."

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:42.229
But he was best known for his TV work.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:47.020
After early appearances in shows like "Mannix"
and "The F.B.I.," Bonner landed the career-defining

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.630
role of Herb Tarlek.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.070
He portrayed the character in all but two
episodes of the series.

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:55.070
His co-star Loni Anderson told the Associated
Press,

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:59.390
"He was one of the funniest men I had the
pleasure of working with, and he was the nicest

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:00.764
man I have ever known."

00:02:00.764 --> 00:02:05.240
Award winning star of stage and screen Hal
Holbrook, best known for his portrayal of

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.890
Mark Twain in his enduring one-man show Mark
Twain Tonight!, died at his California home

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:13.840
in February 2021, his agent told The New York
Times.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:14.840
He was 95.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:20.490
He studied drama at Denison University after
graduating from high school, but World War

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:23.270
II forced him to put his career dreams on
hold.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:27.580
His first on-stage performances took place
when he was stationed in Newfoundland with

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:32.330
the U.S. Army; he joined a theater group and
appeared in a number of local productions.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.930
He began working on what would eventually
become Mark Twain Tonight!

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:42.369
in 1952, and four years later he shot to prominence
after Ed Sullivan invited him onto his variety show.

00:02:42.369 --> 00:02:48.030
Remarkably, Holbrook would go on to perform
as Twain over 2,000 times, including two stints

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.030
on Broadway.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:53.629
The first run scored him a Tony Award, and
he was nominated for an Emmy when Mark Twain

00:02:53.629 --> 00:02:55.629
Tonight! transferred to TV.

00:02:55.629 --> 00:02:59.690
Former child actor Kevin Clark, who played
percussionist turned drummer Freddy Jones

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:05.319
in the Jack Black film "School of Rock," died
after being hit by a motorist in May 2021.

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:10.129
Clark was riding his bicycle in his home city
of Chicago when he was struck by a car.

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:15.050
He was pronounced dead at Illinois Masonic
Medical Center around 45 minutes later.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:16.510
He was 32.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:20.909
Clark landed his part in Richard Linklater's
"School of Rock," his one and only acting

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:24.130
credit, after he tagged along with a friend
to the auditions.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.739
His mother, Allison Clark, told the Chicago
Sun-Times,

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.849
"He just kind of shined.

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:31.930
He took it on right away, but he never really
acted afterward."

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:33.849
He did, however, keep the music up.

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:38.650
Clark went on to play in a number of bands
in the Chicago area and even taught some classes.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.409
Robbie Goldberg, who played with Clark in
the band Robbie Gold, said,

00:03:42.409 --> 00:03:45.379
"He was motivated and loved to write songs,"

00:03:45.379 --> 00:03:49.750
His Hollywood career was short, but Clark
was a stand-out performer in "School of Rock,"

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:54.019
a cult favorite that spawned a Broadway show
and a TV adaptation.

00:03:54.019 --> 00:03:59.250
He was photographed alongside co-star Jack
Black as recently as 2018, and Black said

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.659
in an Instagram tribute,

00:04:00.659 --> 00:04:01.769
"Kevin is gone.

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:03.000
Way too soon.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.110
Beautiful soul.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.360
So many great memories.

00:04:05.493 --> 00:04:06.493
Heartbroken."

00:04:06.827 --> 00:04:12.689
New York-born actor Gregory Sierra, who appeared
in '70s sitcoms Sanford and Son and Barney

00:04:12.689 --> 00:04:15.409
Miller, died of cancer in January 2021.

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:18.200
His widow, Helen Tabor, told CNN,

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.760
"He was doing the best he could and just couldn't
do it anymore.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.870
He was quite wonderful, and my heart is broken
into 400 million pieces."

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.340
He was 83.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:32.470
According to Deadline, Sierra began his career
as a member of the National Shakespeare Company,

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:36.340
appearing in a number of off-Broadway productions
during the 1960s.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:42.244
He made his screen debut in a 1969 episode
of the Robert Wagner spy series, It Takes a Thief.

00:04:42.244 --> 00:04:46.480
He would go on to become a mainstay of the
American TV in the years that followed, popping

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.791
up in numerous sitcoms and cop shows.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:54.160
New York-born actor Yaphet Kotto, who appeared
opposite Roger Moore as a corrupt Caribbean

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:59.270
politician in the James Bond film Live and
Let Die and later played engineer Dennis Parker

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:05.910
in Ridley Scott's 1979 sci-fi flick, Alien
– died in the Philippines in March 2021.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.920
His wife confirmed the news in a Facebook
post writing,

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:12.660
"You played a villain on some of your movies
but for me you're a real hero.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:17.330
A good man, a good father, a good husband
and a decent human being."

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:18.389
He was 81.

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:24.410
Kotto made his movie debut aged 23 in Nothing
but a Man, a film about a Black couple dealing

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:28.000
with racial discrimination in 1960s Alabama.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.810
His sheer size led to him being cast in a
variety of hardman roles in the years that

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:34.810
followed, something that always bothered him.

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:36.550
He once told The Baltimore Sun,

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.300
"I want to try to play a much more sensitive
man.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:42.810
There is an aspect of Black people's lives
that is not running or jumping."

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:47.190
Other notable credits include the Stephen
King adaptation The Running Man, in which

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:52.410
Kotto fought for his freedom alongside Arnold
Schwarzenegger, and the '90s police procedural

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.143
series Homicide: Life on the Street.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.370
British character actor Paul Ritter, who won
praise for his powerful performance in HBO's

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:06.060
Golden Globe-winning miniseries Chernobyl,
died of a brain tumor in April 2021 at the

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.740
age of 54.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:12.060
Ritter was well known in theater communities
on both sides of the Atlantic, having been

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:16.560
nominated for both an Olivier Award and a
Tony Award in his time.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:22.398
He made his on-screen bow in 1992, with an
appearance in the long-running police procedural The Bill.

00:06:22.398 --> 00:06:26.360
He went on to carve out a career as a prolific
TV actor.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.030
Alongside his appearance in Chernobyl, his
best-known performance came in the British

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.229
sitcom Friday Night Dinner, where he played
oddball dad Martin Goodman.

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:39.039
Robert Popper, creator of Fright Night Dinner,
said that he was "devastated at this terribly

00:06:39.039 --> 00:06:41.139
sad news" in a tribute tweet.

00:06:41.139 --> 00:06:44.039
"Paul was a lovely, wonderful human being.

00:06:44.039 --> 00:06:48.199
Kind, funny, super caring and the greatest
actor I ever worked with."

00:06:48.856 --> 00:06:54.949
Comedian and actor Paul Mooney – who regularly
collaborated with Richard Pryor in the 1970s

00:06:54.949 --> 00:06:59.629
and '80s and later became known for his work
on "In Living Color" and "Chappelle's Show"

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:01.690
— died of a heart attack in May 2021.

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:04.120
He was 79.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:08.740
Mooney met Pryor at a party in the late 1960s,
and the pair quickly hit it off.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:13.050
They ended up penning an episode of "Sanford
and Son" together and would collaborate many

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:18.280
more times in the years that followed, including
on the variety series "The Richard Pryor Show,"

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:22.849
the stand-up film "Richard Pryor: Live on
the Sunset Strip," and on Pryor's biographical

00:07:22.849 --> 00:07:25.550
vehicle "Jo Jo Dancer, Your Life Is Calling."

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:29.880
Mooney brought his patented style of comedy
to Keenen Ivory Wayans' "In Living Color"

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:33.410
in the '90s, coming up with characters like
Homey D. Clown.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:38.250
And in the following decade, he became a writer
on another hit sketch show, "Chappelle's Show."

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:42.900
Mooney also appeared on-screen in "Chappelle's
Show," playing characters such as a very opinionated

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.970
movie critic and a Nostradamus parody.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:50.440
Notable big-screen credits include "The Buddy
Holly Story," in which he played seminal singer

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:55.310
Sam Cooke, Spike Lee's "Bamboozled," and the
Mike Epps-led comedy "Meet the Blacks," Mooney's

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:56.389
final film.

00:07:56.627 --> 00:08:01.840
Philadelphia-born actress Marion Ramsey, best
known for playing soft spoken Officer Laverne

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:08.218
Hooks in the Police Academy movies, died in
January 2021, her agency told The Hollywood Reporter.

00:08:08.218 --> 00:08:09.218
The statement read,

00:08:09.218 --> 00:08:15.000
"Marion carried with her a kindness and permeating
light that instantly filled a room upon her arrival.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.410
The dimming of her light is already felt by
those who knew her well.

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.379
We will miss her and always love her."

00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:22.210
No cause of death was released.

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:23.330
She was 73.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:28.569
Ramsey made her first appearance as Officer
Hooks in 1984's Police Academy after spending

00:08:28.569 --> 00:08:30.830
more than a decade in the theatre world.

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:35.190
She remained a mainstay of the wacky comedy
franchise over the coming years, working on

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:36.669
six installments.

00:08:37.116 --> 00:08:42.269
Former child actor Houston Tumlin, who played
Will Ferrell's trash-talking son Walker Bobby

00:08:42.269 --> 00:08:47.839
in 2006 speedway comedy "Talladega Nights:
The Ballad of Ricky Bobby,"died at his home

00:08:47.839 --> 00:08:50.520
in Alabama in March 2021.

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:55.500
The Shelby County coroner confirmed to TMZ
that his death was ruled a suicide.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.740
Tumlin's girlfriend, Charity Robertson, said
in a Facebook post that she would miss the

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:04.100
actor's "big heart, caring spirit [and] infectious
laughter."

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:05.100
She wrote,

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:11.010
"I love you so much Houston Lee, and thank
you for loving me so passionately and unapologetically

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.089
for the time we had each other."

00:09:13.089 --> 00:09:14.769
He was 28.

00:09:14.769 --> 00:09:19.470
Will Ferrell's "Talladega Nights: The Ballad
of Ricky Bobby" was Tumlin's one and only

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:20.470
acting credit.

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:24.660
"I like to picture Jesus as a ninja, fighting
off evil samurai."

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:29.371
The film's director, Adam McKay, said in a
tweet that he was, quote, "truly heartbroken

00:09:29.371 --> 00:09:32.560
and stunned at Houston's passing."

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:33.560
He wrote,

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:34.839
"He was a joyful and talented person.

00:09:34.839 --> 00:09:38.200
Will never forget the laughs and good times
we had.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:41.640
Sending love and prayers to his family and
friends."

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:47.480
If you or anyone you know is having suicidal
thoughts, please dial or text 988 to speak

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.980
with The National Suicide Prevention Lifeline.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:55.055
You can also seek help by visiting 988lifeline.org.

